# Inferential Analytics

We use α = 0.05. If p \< 0.05, reject H₀.

## ANOVA: Environment Satisfaction

-   H₀: Mean morale is equal across environment satisfaction levels.
-   H₁: At least one level has a different mean morale.

```{r anova-env}
anova_env <- aov(Morale_Index ~ Environment_F, data = data)
env_p <- summary(anova_env)[[1]][["Pr(>F)"]][1]
env_f <- summary(anova_env)[[1]][["F value"]][1]
summary(anova_env)
```

Result: F = `r round(env_f, 3)`, p = `r format.pval(env_p)`.

```{r tukey-env}
TukeyHSD(anova_env)
```

## ANOVA: Work-Life Balance

```{r anova-wlb}
anova_wlb <- aov(Morale_Index ~ WorkLife_F, data = data)
wlb_p <- summary(anova_wlb)[[1]][["Pr(>F)"]][1]
summary(anova_wlb)
```

p = `r format.pval(wlb_p)`.

## ANOVA: Relationship Satisfaction

```{r anova-rel}
anova_rel <- aov(Morale_Index ~ Relationship_F, data = data)
rel_p <- summary(anova_rel)[[1]][["Pr(>F)"]][1]
summary(anova_rel)
```

p = `r format.pval(rel_p)`.

## Two-Sample t-test: OverTime

-   H₀: Mean morale is equal for OverTime = Yes and No.
-   H₁: Mean morale differs by overtime status.

```{r ttest-overtime}
t_over <- t.test(Morale_Index ~ OverTime, data = data)
t_over
```

p = `r format.pval(t_over$p.value)`.

## Two-Proportion Test: High Morale in High vs Low Environment

```{r proportion-test}
high_env <- data[data$Environment_F == "Very High", ]
low_env <- data[data$Environment_F == "Low", ]

successes <- c(sum(high_env$High_Morale), sum(low_env$High_Morale))
totals <- c(nrow(high_env), nrow(low_env))
prop_res <- prop.test(successes, totals)
prop_res
```

p = `r format.pval(prop_res$p.value)`.

## Variance Tests

### Levene's Test (Environment groups)

```{r levene-test}
lev_res <- leveneTest(Morale_Index ~ Environment_F, data = data)
lev_res
```

### F-test (OverTime groups)

```{r f-test}
